<!-- MISSION -->
<div class="section section-shepherd fade-up" id="mission">
  <div class="section-label">The Founding</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">The <em>Mandate</em></h2>
  <p class="body-text">
    For eleven years, Rickey Jay Bennett II preached the dignity of labor on the streets of America. Not from a pulpit. Without credentials or institutional backing &#8212; only a Bible and a conviction that work is sacred. That the capacity for productive, dignified labor is not a feature of economic life but a feature of human nature, given by a Creator who worked in the beginning.
  </p>
  <p class="body-text" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
    She preached this from the exact position of someone the economy had left behind. For twenty-three years she navigated homelessness, instability, and a labor market that strips a willing worker of every foothold. She understood from the inside what it means to be willing, to be able, to be trying &#8212; and still to be without.
  </p>
  <p class="body-text" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
    The Thessalonian Mandate Institute is what she built when she stopped waiting for someone else to build it. It was founded on the conviction that the work mandate of scripture is not a comfort. It is a command &#8212; and a command carries with it a corresponding obligation. When technology displaces a worker from their vocation, the community of faith is obligated to equip.
  </p>
  <p class="body-text" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
    The mandate stands. Now we build.
  </p>
</div>

